body:
# PR review

Nothing here looks merge-blocking: I traced every runtime-visible change (the `setPopupOpenState` → `getPopupOpenState` mutate-to-pure refactor, the `Object.keys(updates).length > 0` guard in `useImplicitActiveTrigger`, `activeTriggerId: triggerId ?? null`, the `setIndices` rewrite, and both floating-root sync hooks) and each is behaviour-preserving — `Store.update`'s per-key `Object.is` diff makes the newly-always-present keys no-ops, and no selector distinguishes `undefined` from `null` for `activeTriggerId`. The concerns that remain are about the shape of the fix rather than its correctness: the `const updates = {}` + `as Pick<State, 'k'>` accumulator pattern makes `Store.update` infer `Key = never` at the very call sites this PR rewrote, `getPopupOpenState` gained no direct test despite being the function whose contract changed most, and `Store.update`'s public JSDoc still describes the old loose contract. Verification limit: the PR head is read-only here, so I did not compile the branch — type-level claims below come from reading the signatures, not from `tsc`.

## Tests (2)

### 1. 🟠 `getPopupOpenState` has no direct test, and the fake store in the `applyPopupOpenChange` suite cannot catch its key risk

**Location:** `packages/react/src/utils/popups/popupStoreUtils.ts:180`

```ts
  // If a popup is closing, the `trigger` may be undefined.
  // We want to keep the previous value so that exit animations are played and focus is returned correctly.
  if (triggerId || open) {
    activeTriggerId = triggerId;
    activeTriggerElement = trigger ?? null;
  }
```

This is the function whose contract changed most: it went from mutating a caller-owned object (leaving `preventUnmountingOnClose` / `activeTriggerId` / `activeTriggerElement` *absent* when their branches didn't fire) to returning all four keys, sourced from `state` when the branch doesn't fire. Correctness now depends on the `state` argument being the live store state at write time. It is exported and has four call sites (`DialogStore.ts:97`, `MenuRoot.tsx:297`, `PopoverStore.ts:134`, `popupStoreUtils.ts:270`), but no test calls it directly, and the `applyPopupOpenChange` tests assert only `open`, `openChangeReason`, and `instantType` on the update payload.

**Failure scenario:** Someone later simplifies the fallbacks to literals — e.g. `activeTriggerId: trigger?.id ?? null` unconditionally — and every popup that closes without a trigger (outside press, Escape, programmatic close) loses `activeTriggerElement` immediately, so exit animations lose their anchor and focus is not returned to the trigger. The whole existing suite still passes, because nothing asserts that a close with `trigger === undefined` preserves the previous ids.

**Fix:** Add a small direct `describe('getPopupOpenState')` covering its three branches: (a) `open === true` forces `preventUnmountingOnClose: false`; (b) `open === false, preventUnmountOnClose === true` sets it to `true`; (c) `open === false, trigger === undefined` returns the *previous* `state.activeTriggerId` / `state.activeTriggerElement` rather than `null`.

### 2. 🟡 The new type-only tests don't pin the reshaped popup-helper contracts

**Location:** `packages/react/src/utils/popups/popupStoreUtils.spec.ts:13`

```ts
  usePopupInteractionProps(store, {
    activeTriggerProps: props,
    inactiveTriggerProps: props,
    popupProps: props,
    // @ts-expect-error The store requires a defined item prop bag.
    itemProps: undefined,
  });
```

`ReactStore.spec.ts` is thorough, but the popup-side spec covers exactly one guarantee (explicit `undefined` for an extra key). Two of the three things the new signature buys are unpinned: that omitting one of the three required bags is an error, and that a wrong-typed bag is an error. Separately, the runtime suite's fake store types its mutator as `Partial<OpenChangeState>` (`popupStoreUtils.test.tsx:750`), which satisfies the *old* loose signature just as well as the new one — so `applyPopupOpenChange`'s `update<const Key extends keyof State>(state: Pick<State, Key>)` contract and its `extraState?: Pick<State, ExtraKey>` contract have no compile-time regression coverage anywhere.

**Failure scenario:** A future refactor relaxes `applyPopupOpenChange` back to `update(state: Partial<State>)` / `extraState?: Partial<State>`, reintroducing exactly the `undefined`-into-a-required-field hole issue #5422 describes. `pnpm typescript` stays green and no `@ts-expect-error` flips, so the regression ships silently.

**Fix:** In `popupStoreUtils.spec.ts`, add `@ts-expect-error` cases for a missing `popupProps` and a wrong-typed `itemProps`, plus `applyPopupOpenChange(store, true, details, { extraState: { unknownKey: 1 } })` and a wrong-typed `extraState` value. Type the test double's `update` with the strict generic signature instead of `Partial<OpenChangeState>`.

## Simplifications (2)

### 1. 🟠 The `{}` accumulator + per-key cast pattern makes `update` infer `Key = never`, bypassing the guarantee this PR adds

**Location:** `packages/react/src/toast/store.ts:446`

```ts
    const toastUpdates = {};

    if (clearInteraction) {
      (toastUpdates as Pick<State, 'hovering'>).hovering = false;
      (toastUpdates as Pick<State, 'focused'>).focused = false;
    }
```

The same shape appears at `popupStoreUtils.ts:269` and `:434`, `AriaCombobox.tsx:514`, `toast/store.ts:126`, `useFloatingRootContext.ts:59`, and `useSyncedFloatingRootContext.ts:83` — seven accumulators and roughly fifteen inline `as Pick<State, 'k'>` casts. Because the accumulator's declared type is `{}`, `store.update({ ...acc })` infers `Key = never`, i.e. `Pick<State, never>` = `{}`: the `update` boundary this PR exists to tighten performs zero checking at precisely the call sites the PR rewrote. The key/value guarantee survives only as N hand-written casts, which is strictly weaker (a cast is a manual claim, and one `as any` slips through) and considerably noisier — the accumulator's type no longer says which keys it can hold.

**Failure scenario:** A maintainer adding a field to a batched update writes `(updates as any).activeTriggerId = someTriggerElement` (or misspells the key) in `useImplicitActiveTrigger`; `store.update(updates)` accepts it because `Key` is `never`, an `Element` lands in a `string | null` state field, and `activeTriggerIdSelector` starts returning a DOM node to every trigger that compares against it. That is the same class of defect #5422 reports, reintroduced inside the fix.

**Fix:** Declare the accumulator with its own narrow partial type so values stay checked and the shape is self-documenting, with one cast at the boundary instead of one per assignment:

```ts
const updates: Partial<Pick<State, 'toasts' | 'toastMetadata' | 'hovering' | 'focused'>> = {
  toasts: newToasts,
  toastMetadata: createToastMetadata(newToasts),
};
if (clearInteraction) {
  updates.hovering = false;
  updates.focused = false;
}
this.update(updates as Pick<State, 'toasts' | 'toastMetadata'>);
```

Better still, since conditional batched updates are clearly a recurring need here, add one helper next to `Store.update` (e.g. `updatePartial<const Key extends keyof State>(changes: Partial<Pick<State, Key>>)` that skips `undefined` values) so no call site needs a cast at all, rather than adding fifteen.

### 2. ℹ️ Remaining `Partial<State>` entry points the PR leaves open

**Location:** `packages/react/src/preview-card/store/PreviewCardStore.ts:57`

```ts
  constructor(
    initialState: Partial<State<Payload>>,
```

Every popup store constructor (Dialog, Menu, Popover, PreviewCard, Tooltip) still takes `Partial<State>` and spreads it over the defaults, so an explicit `undefined` can still overwrite a required default — the exact mechanism `update` was tightened against. Likewise `useSyncedFloatingRootContext.ts:79` keeps `floatingId as State['floatingId']`, the one unchecked write left in a file this PR hardened.

**Failure scenario:** No call site hits it today (every `activeTriggerId: defaultTriggerIdProp` defaults to `null`), so this is a follow-up rather than a defect — but the invariant is only as strong as its weakest entry point, and initial state is currently the weakest.

**Fix:** Optional follow-up: narrow the constructors to `Pick<State, Key>` (or an explicit list of overridable keys) in a separate PR, and note in this PR's description that initial state is deliberately out of scope.

## Docs (2)

### 1. 🟠 `Store.update`'s JSDoc still describes the old, looser contract on a published package

**Location:** `packages/utils/src/store/Store.ts:75`

```ts
  /**
   * Merges the provided changes into the current state and notifies listeners if there are changes.
   *
   * @param changes An object containing the changes to apply to the current state.
   */
  update<const Key extends keyof State>(changes: Pick<State, Key>) {
```

"An object containing the changes" no longer describes what is accepted: a `Partial<State>` variable is now rejected, explicit `undefined` for a required field is rejected, and unknown keys are rejected. `@base-ui/utils` is published (v0.3.2) and exports `./store` as a public subpath, so this is a breaking type change for anything outside the monorepo that holds a `Partial<State>` and passes it through — with no in-code guidance on what to do instead. The same applies to `ReactStore.useSyncedValues`.

**Failure scenario:** An external consumer upgrades, `store.update(myPartialState)` stops compiling with an opaque `Pick<State, K>` inference error, and neither the JSDoc nor a changelog entry explains that the value must be narrowed to an exact known subset first.

**Fix:** Extend the `@param` to state the contract explicitly — values must match their key's type, no unknown keys, no explicit `undefined` for a required field — and add the one-line migration note from the PR description ("represent broad partials as an exact known subset before writing"). Make sure the release notes flag it as a breaking type change for `@base-ui/utils`.

### 2. 🟡 New comment is spliced into the middle of an unrelated comment block

**Location:** `packages/react/src/utils/popups/popupStoreUtils.ts:317`

```ts
  // `stateUpdates` is key/value checked at this hook's boundary. TypeScript does not preserve that
  // generic `Pick` relationship after object spreads, so the merged state objects are asserted below.
  // Applies trigger-owned state (active-trigger ownership and payload) when the trigger registers.
  // Stable so payload/`stateUpdates` changes do not change the ref identity (which would needlessly
  // churn registration); it reads the latest closure values when invoked.
  const applyTriggerData = useStableCallback((element: Element) => {
```

The two new lines were prepended to the existing block documenting `applyTriggerData`, so the comment now opens with a note about type assertions and only then explains what the callback does and why it is stable. It also over-reaches: it explains casts in `applyTriggerData` *and* in the unrelated `useIsoLayoutEffect` further down, from a position attached to neither.

**Failure scenario:** A reader scanning for "what does `applyTriggerData` do" hits a TypeScript aside first, and someone editing the effect at `:363` has no comment explaining why its `as Pick<...>` cast is there.

**Fix:** Leave the `applyTriggerData` block as it was and put the type note on the assertions it explains — either a single line above each `as Pick<Readonly<State>, ...>` or, if it should be stated once, in the function's JSDoc next to the `stateUpdates` `@param`.

## Verdict

**Approve after nits** - no runtime regression survived tracing all four behavioural refactors, but the accumulator pattern bypasses the guarantee at its own call sites, `getPopupOpenState` ships untested, and the public `Store.update` contract change is undocumented.

